WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.330 --> 00:04.450
So what is debiasing, and why is it so important
when defining a problem?

2
00:04.930 --> 00:08.320
So debiasing is taking a very structured approach

3
00:08.650 --> 00:13.600
to think about what are some of the biases that may come into
the way we define a problem up front,

4
00:13.600 --> 00:19.400
and think about what we can do as problem-solving leaders
to try to take that bias out of the situation.

5
00:19.400 --> 00:21.190
Everyone has biases.

6
00:21.770 --> 00:27.330
It's interesting — we often think that others have biases,
but we ourselves don't.

7
00:28.100 --> 00:32.070
And it's interesting that when you do problem solving in the moment,

8
00:32.650 --> 00:37.600
your biases are not always at the forefront of your thinking.

9
00:37.960 --> 00:43.200
Put differently, biases are actually oftentimes good, right?
Because they're heuristics — they're human heuristics.

10
00:43.200 --> 00:48.600
They're shortcuts, so that we know when a lion is running towards us,
we don't have to actively problem-solve.

11
00:48.600 --> 00:52.600
We understand that, hey, the shortcut is: we've got to run.

12
00:52.770 --> 00:55.910
So biases are intrinsically there to protect us.

13
00:56.230 --> 01:00.400
However, we actually need to be aware of them as business leaders

14
01:00.400 --> 01:06.210
to really make sure that we're not reconfirming something
that has historically happened but may not happen again.

15
01:06.530 --> 01:12.160
So what I often find very common in a lot of problem solving
is that we actually have

16
01:12.490 --> 01:18.820
confirmation bias: we're all actually looking just to confirm
whatever finding or thought that we had before.

17
01:19.330 --> 01:25.860
Or we have groupthink — because the leader thinks something,
therefore it must be right, and we all just kind of fall in line.

18
01:26.250 --> 01:32.000
The most effective way for us to actually try and debias a bit
is to sometimes have a challenger session —

19
01:32.680 --> 01:34.500
your organisation might call it something different.

20
01:35.210 --> 01:40.830
It's to actually take whoever is a strong leader
or a voice in one perspective,

21
01:41.160 --> 01:44.230
and see if they can argue the other perspective. And vice versa.

22
01:45.060 --> 01:48.610
It's sometimes a little bit funny. It can feel a little bit contrived.

23
01:48.930 --> 01:53.220
But it's an incredibly powerful way for us to make sure
that we're actually checking our blind spots,

24
01:53.770 --> 01:58.910
and that we're actually challenging ourselves also to be
a bit more empathetic as leaders in our problem solving —

25
01:59.240 --> 02:05.220
so that we're not so grounded and married in our own perspectives
that we can't actually see what our counterparts might be thinking.
